{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "weno5_eno1_signed_norm_D2_extrapolate_bc",
    "description": "Hybrid WENO5/ENO1 gradient magnitude |grad u| = sqrt(u_x^2 + u_y^2) for the level-set REINITIALISATION equation psi_tau = S(psi_0) (1 - |grad psi|) of NCAR fire_behavior (physics/level_set_mod.F90 Advance_ls_reinit, fire_upwinding_reinit = 4; Sussman, Smereka & Osher 1994 as adapted by Munoz-Esparza et al. 2018), on cartesian_uniform_2d with the same linear-extrapolation halo closure as rules/weno5_eno1_norm_D2_extrapolate_bc. It differs from that rule in exactly one respect, which is entirely inside the two interior stencils it imports: the upwind side is chosen by a supplied sign field `upwind_sign` (fire_behavior's lfn_s0, the smoothed sign S(psi_0) = psi_0 / sqrt(psi_0^2 + dx^2) frozen at the start of the pseudo-time iteration) rather than by the wind-relative propagation sign, and the band test that selects WENO5 over ENO1 is SIGNED rather than absolute -- see stencils/weno5_eno1_signed_D1x_interior for both, including why the missing abs() is reproduced rather than corrected. CONSEQUENCE FOR A CONSUMER: this rule requires the free name `upwind_sign` ([x, y]) and does NOT reference `wind_u`/`wind_v`, so a model that imports it must not also import the propagation norm rule -- they match the same sqrt-of-sum-of-squares pattern at the same priority 20 and would be ambiguous. ORDER: tagged order:1 for the same reason as its sibling -- the ENO1 branch and the wall closure are first-order, and the fifth-order branch applies only inside the band, so no whole-field convergence claim is made and no convergence case is shipped. Free names: upwind_sign, bdy_eno1_cells, weno_band_cells, dx, dy. Requires NX, NY >= 7.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:rule",
      "family:finite_difference",
      "grid:cartesian_uniform_2d",
      "op:grad_norm",
      "axes:x,y",
      "order:1",
      "stencil_width:7",
      "bc:extrapolate_linear",
      "spec:9.6.8"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Munoz-Esparza, D., Kosovic, B., Jimenez, P. A., & Coen, J. L. (2018). An accurate fire-spread algorithm in the Weather Research and Forecasting model using the level-set method. Journal of Advances in Modeling Earth Systems, 10(4), 908-926. The hybrid WENO5/ENO1 level-set scheme (fire_upwinding = 9) of WRF-Fire / NCAR fire_behavior.",
        "doi": "10.1002/2017MS001108"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Jiang, G.-S., & Shu, C.-W. (1996). Efficient implementation of weighted ENO schemes. Journal of Computational Physics, 126(1), 202-228. The classical WENO5 smoothness indicators beta_k and nonlinear weights w_k = gamma_k/(beta_k + eps)^2.",
        "doi": "10.1006/jcph.1996.0130"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Harten, A., Engquist, B., Osher, S., & Chakravarthy, S. R. (1987). Uniformly high order accurate essentially non-oscillatory schemes, III. Journal of Computational Physics, 71(2), 231-303. ENO stencil selection by smallest divided difference (first-order form: the minmod limiter).",
        "doi": "10.1016/0021-9991(87)90031-3"
      },
      {
        "citation": "Osher, S., & Sethian, J. A. (1988). Fronts propagating with curvature-dependent speed: Algorithms based on Hamilton-Jacobi formulations. Journal of Computational Physics, 79(1), 12-49.",
        "doi": "10.1016/0021-9991(88)90002-2"
      },
      {
        "citation": "NCAR Community Fire Behavior Model (fire_behavior), develop @ eb77580, physics/level_set_mod.F90: Calc_tend_ls, Select_eno, Select_weno5, Select_4th, Select_godunov (case 4), Extrapol_var_at_bdys. The transcribed reference implementation."
      }
    ]
  },
